Design Foundation Session
The framework — the spine you run every time
Nine beats, and in a 45-minute round you roughly spend 5 min scoping, 10 min on the high-level, 20 min on interviewer-steered deep dives, 10 min on scale/reliability/wrap:

Scope & constraints first — always open here. Ask: how many models × backends × arches, request scale, SLOs (TTFT/availability), who's the user (internal teams? enterprise? air-gapped?), build cadence (day-0?). Then state your assumptions out loud. Skipping this is the #1 senior-candidate failure.
Requirements — separate functional from non-functional (latency, throughput, cost, reliability, security) and prioritize them.
High-level architecture — assemble the boxes from the building blocks below; draw control flow and data flow.
Deep-dive the hard part — pick the 1–2 that actually matter; let the interviewer steer, then go deep.
Scale & bottlenecks — "where does this break at 10×?"
Reliability & failure modes — what fails, blast radius, rollback.
Security & multi-tenancy — isolation, supply chain.
Observability & day-2 ops — SLIs, upgrades, canary.
Tradeoffs & wrap — summarize decisions and name what you'd revisit.

The meta-skill: you drive. Narrate, structure, make decisions and justify them. A design round is a conversation you lead, not a Q&A you endure.
The building blocks — learn these once, snap them together
Each is a reusable primitive. I'm giving you the key considerations and the signature phrases that signal seniority — memorize the phrases.
1. Control plane (CRD + Operator). The declarative brain: users declare desired NIMs via a CRD, the operator reconciles to converge (this is your coding #3). NVIDIA actually ships a NIM Operator with CRDs like NIMService/NIMCache. Considerations: CRD schema + versioning, idempotent reconcile, rollout strategy, status reporting, finalizers for cleanup.
Say: "separate control plane from data plane," "level-triggered reconcile diffing only owned fields to avoid hot loops," "the operator is the API, Helm is just packaging."
2. Build farm (BuildKit + cache + multi-arch). Produces the images. Parallel builders, registry-backed remote cache, multi-arch (x86 + Grace/arm), CUDA-optimized. Considerations: the build matrix (model × backend × arch × CUDA version), reproducibility, multi-stage builds, base-image/layer strategy (your coding #1).
Say: "registry-backed BuildKit cache so cache hits survive across ephemeral runners," "multi-stage builds keep toolchains out of the runtime image," "stable layers low, volatile layers high, to maximize cache reuse and cross-image dedup," "reproducible builds via pinned digests."
3. Artifact store (registry). Content-addressed store for images and weights. Considerations: dedup via content-addressing, GC of unreferenced blobs, geo-replication, pull-through caching, and — critically — model weights as separate artifacts, not baked into the image.
Say: "weights are huge and versioned independently, so I store them as separate OCI artifacts or in a model registry and keep the container image small and cache-friendly," "content-addressed dedup across the whole model×backend matrix."
4. Distribution (bits → nodes, fast). This is the cold-start bottleneck. Considerations: lazy/seekable pulling (SOCI, eStargz, nydus) so the container starts before the full pull completes; node-level weight caching (pre-pull DaemonSet, PVC, NIMCache); P2P distribution (Dragonfly/Kraken) to avoid registry hot-spotting; air-gap mirroring.
Say: "startup is dominated by image pull + weight load, so I decouple them — lazy-pull the image and pre-stage weights on a fast local cache," "P2P distribution so a 500-replica scale-up doesn't melt the registry."
5. Runtime placement (onto GPUs). Considerations: device plugin advertises GPUs; MIG (hard partitions, fixed profiles), time-slicing (bursty), MPS (cooperative); NVLink/topology awareness; bin-packing (your coding #6); taints/tolerations and node pools.
Say: "match the sharing mechanism to the workload — MIG for hard isolation and predictable QoS, time-slicing for bursty low-priority, MPS for cooperative," "bin-pack to keep whole GPUs free for large models, and respect NVLink topology for multi-GPU serving."
6. Autoscaler. Right-sizes replicas (your coding #6). Considerations: custom metric (KV-cache utilization, queue depth, or an SLO like TTFT) via a Prometheus adapter or KEDA; scale-from-zero; cluster autoscaler for nodes.
Say: "scale on the real limiter — KV-cache utilization or queue depth — not GPU util," "scale-from-zero needs a request-triggered activator because you can't infer load at zero," "aggressive up, conservative down, because cold start is expensive."
7. Observability. Considerations: build metrics (build time, cache-hit rate, image size); runtime SLIs (TTFT, ITL, throughput, KV-cache util, queue depth); DCGM exporter for GPU telemetry; tracing; SLOs.
Say: "instrument the SLIs that map to user pain (TTFT, ITL) and to capacity (KV-cache, queue depth) — the capacity ones double as autoscaling signals," "measure before you optimize" (that last one is Donghan's exact wavelength).
8. Supply-chain security. Considerations: SBOM generation, signing (cosign/sigstore), provenance/attestation (SLSA), CVE scanning, admission-time signature verification, encrypted proprietary weights.
Say: "sign and attest at build, verify at admission — unbroken provenance from source to serving," "enterprise and air-gapped customers need SBOMs, signed images, and CVE SLAs," "encrypt proprietary weights, decrypt only inside the trusted runtime."
Handling pushback
When they challenge you, it's usually an invitation to go deeper, not a "gotcha." Don't get defensive. Acknowledge the tradeoff, reason to a decision, and say what you'd measure to confirm: "Fair — that trades startup latency for storage cost; I'd lean toward X because of our SLO, but I'd validate with the cache-hit rate." If you genuinely don't know something, reason from first principles out loud rather than bluffing — with Jason (ex-Meta) and Adam (principal architect), first-principles reasoning beats fake NVIDIA-specific confidence every time.
